---
/**
 * The end-of-page "Next" list, laid out as cards — `{% linkcards %}`.
 *
 * IT TAKES A MARKDOWN LIST, NOT ATTRIBUTES, and that is the whole reason it exists beside the
 * Starlight preset's `{% cardgrid %}` + `{% linkcard %}` pair. Those ask an author for two tag names
 * and three attributes per card; the pages in this tree already END with exactly the list this
 * component wants, so adopting it is a two-line change to a finished page rather than a rewrite:
 *
 *   {% linkcards %}
 *   - [Sources](/connections/sources/): the connection a Space reads through
 *   - [Reconnecting](/connections/reconnecting/): what to do when one breaks
 *   {% /linkcards %}
 *
 * Every card is a link followed by a sentence. There is no title-only card and no card without a
 * destination, because a "Next" block whose entries do not go anywhere is a list of nouns.
 *
 * WHY `is:global` AND A PREFIX. The list is SLOTTED CONTENT: Astro stamps it with the hash of the
 * page that wrote it, not this component's, so a scoped rule here matches nothing at all — valid
 * CSS, shipped, zero elements selected. `bolc-` is the unique prefix that keeps a global sheet from
 * reaching anything else.
 *
 * WHY EVERY MARGIN IS RESET EXPLICITLY. Starlight's prose rhythm is a general-sibling rule that
 * margins every child of every nested box, including the ones inside these cards. Left alone it
 * pushes the description away from its own title and the grid rows out of alignment.
 */
---

<div class="bolc">
  <slot />
</div>

<style is:global>
  /* One column below 640, two above it. `auto-fit` rather than a fixed count: three "Next" entries
     and two "Next" entries are both common, and a fixed count leaves the odd one stretched across a
     row of its own at a width nothing else in the column uses. */
  .bolc ul {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: 12px;
    margin: 24px 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .bolc ul li {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* The rhythm rule again, one level down: a tight Markdown list gives bare text, a loose one gives
     `<p>`, and the same page can produce both. */
  .bolc ul li > * {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .bolc ul li > * + * {
    margin-top: 4px;
  }

  /* The link is the card's title and the card's whole hit area. `::after` stretched over the `li`
     is what makes the sentence beneath it clickable too, without nesting anything inside the
     anchor — the description often carries its own inline links, and an anchor inside an anchor is
     not valid HTML. */
  .bolc ul li a:first-child {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-16);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .bolc ul li a:first-child::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    border-radius: inherit;
  }

  .bolc ul li {
    position: relative;
  }

  .bolc ul li:hover {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  .bolc ul li:hover a:first-child {
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  /* The stretched `::after` sits above the description's own links, so any link that is not the
     title has to be lifted back out to stay clickable. */
  .bolc ul li a:not(:first-child) {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  /* Keyboard reaches the title link, and the ring has to appear on the card rather than on the
     invisible stretched box. */
  .bolc ul li:focus-within {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
</style>
